## 6. Audio import and recording management

Support:

- Importing audio files through a file picker
- Drag-and-drop audio import
- Importing recordings from the ESP32
- Viewing recording duration and file size
- Playing audio through a local audio player
- Renaming recordings
- Editing recording metadata
- Deleting recordings with confirmation
- Reprocessing recordings
- Viewing processing history
- Exporting original recordings
- Recovering interrupted imports

Preserve each recording's identity and original creation time where available.

Use unique stable IDs, not filenames alone.

Prevent duplicate imports by comparing recording IDs when available and using file fingerprints or other suitable duplicate detection when needed. Duplicate detection should suggest matches rather than silently deleting or merging recordings.

Never delete a recording from the SD card merely because an upload was attempted. The device and app should use explicit transfer acknowledgements and clearly defined retention behavior.

## 7. ESP32 local synchronization

Design a hardware integration layer that can work with the existing ESP32 firmware.

Do not assume a particular firmware API, IP address, file format, or transfer protocol without inspecting the existing implementation or documenting the assumption.

If the firmware is available, inspect it and adapt the application to the actual protocol. If it is not available, define a clean integration interface that can be configured later.

The application should support:

- Discovering or connecting to the device through a supported local Wi-Fi mechanism
- Manually entering the device address when discovery is unavailable
- Requesting a list of recordings
- Displaying filenames, recording IDs, timestamps, durations, and transfer status
- Selecting recordings for import
- Showing transfer progress
- Retrying interrupted transfers
- Resuming partial transfers if the device supports them
- Detecting duplicate imports
- Confirming successful local storage
- Showing recordings that remain on the SD card
- Displaying device and synchronization errors
- Maintaining an import history

Prefer a local HTTP API or another practical local transfer mechanism compatible with the existing firmware.

Account for browser security restrictions on local-network requests, CORS, permissions, mixed-content restrictions, and HTTPS requirements. Provide a documented manual-import fallback if direct browser-to-device communication is unavailable in a target browser.

Do not claim that seamless device discovery works in every browser.

## 8. Audio bookmarks and tap-to-replay

Implement audio bookmarks as a first-class feature.

A bookmark identifies an important point in a recording and can originate from either the ESP32's physical buttons or the web app.

Each bookmark should store:

- Stable bookmark ID
- Recording ID
- Timestamp in milliseconds
- Optional label
- Optional color or category
- Creation source: device or application
- Optional linked transcript segment
- Creation time

If the ESP32 firmware can mark timestamps during recording, integrate those markers with the imported audio. If the current firmware does not support them, define the metadata contract and provide a way to add bookmarks in the app.

The audio player must include:

- Play and pause
- Seek bar
- Current time and total duration
- Playback speed controls
- Skip backward and forward
- Bookmark markers on the timeline
- A bookmark list
- Add, rename, and delete bookmark actions
- Jump directly to a bookmark
- Highlight the active bookmark when seeking
- Keyboard-accessible playback controls

### Transcript synchronization

When a transcript contains timestamps, display transcript segments in chronological order.

Clicking a timestamped segment must seek the audio player to the corresponding position.

While audio is playing, highlight the currently active transcript segment when reliable timing data is available.

Allow users to create bookmarks from transcript segments and navigate from bookmarks back to the relevant transcript.

Preserve timestamps when editing transcript text. If the text is changed, keep the original segment boundaries unless the user explicitly edits the timing or requests realignment.

Never fabricate precise sentence timestamps if the transcription service did not provide suitable timing data. When precise alignment is unavailable, use clearly labeled approximate timing or provide playback without transcript highlighting.

## 9. The Smart Inbox

The Inbox is the primary destination for newly imported recordings.

Every recording should have a visible lifecycle status, for example:

- Imported
- Awaiting transcription
- Transcribing
- Awaiting organization
- Ready for review
- Filed
- Failed
- Archived

The Inbox should surface:

- New recordings
- Incomplete transcripts
- Incomplete summaries
- Failed processing jobs
- Notes without a project
- Possible duplicates
- Suggested related notes
- Suggested projects
- Pending task approvals

Allow bulk actions such as assigning a project, applying tags, archiving notes, retrying processing, and marking notes as reviewed.

Bulk operations must show a clear confirmation or undo mechanism where appropriate.

A recording must not disappear from the Inbox simply because an AI request failed.

## 10. The organizer: core product logic

Build a structured organizer rather than a folder system that allows arbitrary nesting and uncontrolled AI-generated categories.

Keep these concepts separate:

**Inbox:** new and unreviewed material.

**Notes:** the actual editable content associated with a recording or manually created note.

**Projects:** persistent collections of notes around a longer-term goal or subject.

**Tags:** reusable labels that can apply across multiple projects.

**Saved views / Sections:** dynamic filters such as Today's Notes, Meeting Notes, Ideas, Lectures, Unfinished, Bookmarks, or Recently Edited.

**Archive:** a status that hides older or completed notes from the main active views without removing them from search.

Do not create a new folder or project merely because a note has a new title.

### Organizer pipeline

After a recording is transcribed:

1. Generate a title and summary.
2. Identify the likely note type.
3. Extract key points and action items.
4. Suggest reusable tags.
5. Compare the note against existing projects.
6. Retrieve potentially related existing notes.
7. Detect possible duplicate recordings or notes.
8. Produce organization suggestions.
9. Save the results locally.
10. Present uncertain decisions for user review.

### Project selection rules

Provide the organizer with the actual available project IDs, names, and descriptions.

Prefer an existing project when the note clearly fits.

Allow the AI to suggest a new project, but do not create one automatically when an existing project is a plausible match.

If confidence is low, leave the note in the Inbox and ask the user.

Never let the model invent project IDs, note IDs, or other database identifiers. Validate all references against the local database before saving them.

Manual user decisions always take precedence over AI suggestions.

### User-friendly review screen

For every newly processed recording, show:

- Editable title
- Summary preview
- Suggested note type
- Suggested project
- Suggested tags
- Related notes
- Extracted action items
- Possible duplicates
- Processing warnings, if any

Provide actions to:

- Accept all safe suggestions
- Change the project
- Add or remove tags
- Approve or reject action items
- Open related notes
- Keep in Inbox
- Save and file
- Archive
- Retry processing

Support an optional setting for automatically filing high-confidence matches. Conservative review should be the default.

### Duplicate and related-note logic

Related notes and duplicate notes are different concepts.

A duplicate detector should identify likely copies of the same recording or content.

A related-note system should find useful connections between distinct notes that discuss similar ideas.

Never automatically merge separate notes based only on semantic similarity.

Start with local text search, shared tags, title similarity, and metadata. Later, support semantic embeddings and a local similarity index.

Allow users to confirm, dismiss, or manually create relationships.

