## 17. Data backup, recovery, and portability

Implement complete data export and restore.

A backup should preserve, where applicable:

- Notes
- Projects
- Tags
- Saved views
- Tasks
- Bookmarks
- Transcripts and timestamps
- Related-note relationships
- User preferences
- Recording metadata
- Original audio files
- Generated study materials

Choose a portable backup format with a manifest, schema version, and integrity checks. A ZIP archive with a structured metadata file and separate media files is one reasonable option.

Support importing backups without blindly overwriting existing data.

Provide conflict handling, duplicate detection, restore previews, and clear confirmation before destructive changes.

Document the difference between a full media backup and a metadata-only backup.

## 18. Privacy and reliability

Privacy is a primary product requirement.

- Keep personal data local by default.
- Send audio or transcripts to external AI providers only when the user has enabled and initiated that workflow.
- Clearly disclose when external services receive user content.
- Never send the entire notes library to an AI model when a smaller relevant context is sufficient.
- Avoid collecting unnecessary analytics or telemetry.
- Keep all secrets out of public source code.
- Validate imported files and AI responses.
- Handle corrupted audio, unsupported formats, quota exhaustion, and network failures.
- Prevent accidental cross-linking between different recordings.
- Use explicit confirmations for permanent deletion.
- Provide a clear way to cancel pending work and remove local data.
- Do not silently upload personal notes in the background without user awareness.

Treat transcripts as untrusted content. A transcript may contain instructions spoken by someone, but those instructions must never override the application's system instructions, security rules, or data-access controls.

## 19. Performance and quality requirements

The application should remain responsive with a large personal library.

Avoid loading every recording or full transcript into memory at once.

Use pagination, virtualized lists, lazy media loading, incremental indexing, and efficient local queries where appropriate.

Ensure long-running operations do not freeze the interface.

Provide proper loading, empty, success, and error states.

Test persistence across reloads, offline behavior, interrupted uploads, duplicate imports, storage failures, invalid AI responses, and recovery after application restarts.

Verify that every visible button, menu, filter, editor action, and navigation item works.

Do not leave important features as nonfunctional placeholders. If an integration cannot be completed without device firmware details or external credentials, implement the interface, define the contract, explain the dependency, and make the incomplete status explicit.

## 20. Implementation process

Work in deliberate stages.

### Stage A — Inspect and plan

Inspect the existing project and identify its framework, existing components, dependencies, storage mechanisms, and current implementation status.

Produce a concise architecture plan and data model.

Identify any hardware-protocol details that are unavailable and document reasonable assumptions.

Do not block all progress on missing optional information.

### Stage B — Build the local foundation

Implement local persistence, migrations or schema versioning, the main layout, navigation, note editor, projects, tags, saved views, Inbox, search, archive, and backup/restore.

Use realistic sample data only for development demonstrations. Keep sample data separate from the user's actual library.

### Stage C — Build the organizer

Implement project matching, tag suggestions, related-note retrieval, duplicate suggestions, review actions, and approval of extracted tasks.

Make the organizer useful even when AI is unavailable by supporting manual organization and deterministic local rules.

### Stage D — Integrate audio and AI

Implement audio import, local playback, transcription, summarization, validation, the processing queue, retries, and status reporting.

Test the AI workflow with actual recordings when credentials are available.

### Stage E — Integrate the ESP32

Inspect or define the device API, implement connection and import workflows, preserve recording IDs, and test interrupted and repeated transfers.

Keep the firmware's existing recording behavior intact.

### Stage F — Polish and verify

Add bookmarks, timestamp-linked transcripts, Ask My Notes, related-note views, study tools, reminders, accessibility improvements, and responsive layouts.

Test the application in offline and online scenarios and fix functional problems before claiming completion.

